{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims the panel wins only under a restricted definition of reliability (variance reduction), but then offers no evidence within a single evaluation that would distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. The problem explicitly asks the respondent to 'identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer.' SRC_001's mechanism and counterconditions address variance and shared bias but never specify any intra-evaluation evidential criterion (e.g., item-level agreement, out-of-checklist probe, or calibration pattern) that separates real understanding from rote compliance, so it leaves the mandatory evidential task unaddressed.","target":"41cbcdcef491f47deaf6ea24c7877243862097d5117a0fe67bf394b745ed67a9"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 identifies the 'Checklist-Intuition Gap' and two counterconditions, but it does not supply the intra-evaluation evidence the problem demands for separating a genuine reliability gain from compliance that merely tracks correct answers. Its mechanism describes when the panel wins or loses, not how a single evaluation would reveal whether the panel's correctness reflects genuine reliability rather than checklist-conformant pattern matching. It thus answers the comparative claim while omitting the second, evidential requirement of the problem.","target":"65cee4454ff66da1822dafc212dd8415c9a7f9dbd2aa2cea7e8bc3e5ea08294e"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03 argues the panel is less reliable due to homogenized error, but it assumes that training creates a 'synthetic intuition' that eliminates independence without offering intra-evaluation evidence for the required distinction. The problem asks what evidence within a single evaluation distinguishes genuine reliability gain from checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Even SRC_03's counterconditions about training content and background diversity are panel-composition moderators, not evidential tests; it never identifies a within-evaluation signal (such as correlated errors on checklist gaps, or divergent performance on untrained probes) that would operationalize the homogenization concern, so it fails to satisfy the evidential half of the question.","target":"e31f362c20e3d13a9717aca7d808e27a7142743a1b0a80fdb7c63c713ab9acf5"}]}